"You type an address, press Enter, and a page appears. It feels as if the page were already inside your computer. It is not. It is stored on another computer, perhaps in another country, and your computer has to ask for it. Opening a web page is a short conversation between two machines.": "fc451841ed87",
"You type an address, press Enter, and a page appears. It feels as if the page were already inside your computer. It is not. It is stored on another computer, perhaps in another country, and your computer has to ask for it. Opening a web page is a short conversation between two machines. The two machines have names. Yours is the client: your computer or phone, and the web browser on it. The other is a server: a computer that stores web pages and sends them out when asked. The client asks. The server answers. That is the pattern of the whole web. First there is a small problem. You typed a name, such as example.com, but computers find each other by number. So the browser asks the Domain Name System, or DNS. It works like an address book: give it a name, and it gives back a number called an IP address, which looks something like 192.0.2.10. Now the browser knows where to send its question. Next, the browser sends the server a request. It is a short message that says, in effect, “please send me this page.” The server answers with a code and, if all is well, the page. The code 200 means OK. The code 404 means “not found”: the server is there, but it has no page at that address. And what is the page? It is a file of text, written in a language called HTML. Tags in the text say what each piece is: this is a heading, that is a paragraph. The browser reads the text and draws the page from it. If the page has pictures, the browser asks for each of them in the same way, one request after another. The web is not the same thing as the internet. The internet is the worldwide network that connects the computers, and the web is one of the services that run on it. Email and instant messaging are others. Each time you tap a link, the same conversation happens again: a name, a number, a request, an answer, and a page drawn from text.": "5f9374eadc83",
